QB.add(58,[{"n":1,"q":"A 30-year-old man from North America presents to the emergency department with rapidly progressing muscle weakness over the past two weeks. He reports having had a mild gastrointestinal illness a few weeks prior. On examination, he exhibits areflexia and difficulty with eye movements. Electrophysiological studies indicate demyelination. Which of the following variants of Guillain-Barré Syndrome is most likely associated with this patient's presentation?","o":[{"t":"Acute inflammatory demyelinating polyneuropathy (AIDP)"},{"t":"Axonal variant"},{"t":"Miller Fisher Syndrome"},{"t":"Chronic inflammatory demyelinating polyneuropathy (CIDP)"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Answer: A) Acute inflammatory demyelinating polyneuropathy (AIDP)"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Explanation:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Acute inflammatory demyelinating polyneuropathy (AIDP) is the most common variant of GBS in Europe and North America and is often preceded by infections, aligning with the patient’s recent gastrointestinal illness."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Causes of Guillain-Barré Syndrome (GBS):"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Aspect Details"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Definition A group of immune-mediated conditions causing acute"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"paralysis, with the most common variant being acute inflammatory demyelinating polyneuropathy (AIDP)."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Incidence 1–2 cases per 100,000 individuals per year."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Common Preceding About two-thirds of AIDP cases have a prior history of Infections infection, often triggering an autoimmune response"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"leading to demyelination."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Variants of Guillain-Barré Syndrome:"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Variant Description"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Acute Inflammatory Demyelinating The most common variant is in Europe & North America. Polyneuropathy (AIDP) Patchy segmental demyelination of spinal cord and"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"peripheral nerves Antibodies seen: IgG Anti GM1 IgG Anti GQ1b"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Axonal Variants More prevalent in China and Japan; account for 10% of (Option B ruled out) GBS cases in Western countries; often associated with"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Campylobacter jejuni infection."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Miller Fisher Syndrome Best-recognised variant. (Option C ruled out) Associated with anti-GQ1b antibodies."},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Presents with internal and external ophthalmoplegia, ataxia, and areflexia."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Clinical Course:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Weakness progresses over a maximum of 4 weeks (usually less)."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Can lead to respiratory failure within hours."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Chronic inflammatory demyelinating polyneuropathy (CIDP) (Option D): A chronic condition not consistent with the acute presentation observed in this patient."}]},{"n":2,"q":"A recent study on autoantibodies associated with Guillain-Barré syndrome (GBS) revealed important findings. Which of the following statements about the role of autoantibodies in GBS is TRUE?","o":[{"t":"Anti-ganglioside antibodies are rarely found in GBS patients and have no prognostic significance."},{"t":"The presence of anti-GM1 antibodies correlates with better clinical outcomes in GBS patients."},{"t":"A diverse array of autoantibodies targeting nerve components suggests multiple immune mechanisms contribute to GBS pathology."},{"t":"Specific autoantibodies are only directed against gangliosides, with no other targets identified in GBS pathology."}],"a":[2],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Answer: C) A diverse array of autoantibodies targeting nerve components suggests multiple immune mechanisms contribute to GBS pathology."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Explanation:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Patients with Guillain-Barré syndrome (GBS) produce various autoantibodies that attack different components of the nervous system."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"The presence of these multiple autoantibodies indicates that the underlying immune response is not uniform; it involves different mechanisms that can lead to nerve damage."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"This diversity suggests that GBS may arise from a complex interplay of immune processes rather than a single pathway, contributing to the variability in clinical presentations and outcomes among patients."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Autoantibodies in Guillain-Barré Syndrome (GBS):"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Aspect Details"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Diversity of Autoantibodies GBS patients exhibit a wide range of autoantibodies"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"targeting nerve cells and structures, indicating multiple immune mechanisms may contribute to GBS pathology."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Prevalence of Anti-Gangliosid Anti-ganglioside antibodies are the most common , e Antibodies detected in up to 50% of GBS patients, often arising (Option A ruled out) from microbial molecular mimicry."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Prognostic Implications Specific autoantibodies, especially anti-GM1 , are (Option B ruled out) associated with poorer clinical outcomes."},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Presence correlates with variants like pure motor variants and Miller-Fisher syndrome."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Target Antigens While gangliosides are primary targets, other potential (Option D ruled out) targets include nodal and paranodal proteins (e.g.,"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"neurofascin), suggesting a broader spectrum of autoantibody involvement."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Clinical-Immunological Research aims to establish correlations between GBS Correlations clinical presentations and specific autoantibodies,"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"enhancing understanding of immune responses in relation to patient outcomes."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Conclusion:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"The study emphasizes the complexity of autoantibody involvement in GBS, suggesting that understanding these immune markers could enhance diagnosis and treatment strategies for affected individuals."}]},{"n":3,"q":"Which of the following statements is incorrect regarding the types of Guillain-Barré syndrome?","o":[{"t":"AIDP is the most common form in adults."},{"t":"AMSAN has a better prognosis than AMAN."},{"t":"AMAN involves axonal degeneration in children and young adults"},{"t":"Miller-Fisher syndrome is associated with anti-GQ1b antibodies"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Answer: B) AMSAN has a better prognosis than AMAN."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Explanation:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"AMSAN typically has a worse prognosis than AMAN"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Type Features Neural Electrodiagnostic Study"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Target"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"AIDP (Acute Inflammatory Most common in adults Schwann Demyelination Demyelinating (Option A) cells Polyneuropathy) Rapid recovery"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"IgG Anti-GM1 a/b"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"AMAN (Acute Motor Seen in children and Nodes of Axonal degeneration Axonal Neuropathy) young adults (Option C) Ranvier"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Poor prognosis IgG Anti-GM1 a/b"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"AMSAN (Acute Motor Uncommon Nodes of Axonal degeneration Sensory Axonal Seen in adults Ranvier Neuropathy) Worst prognosis"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"IgG Anti-GM1 a/b"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Miller Fisher Syndrome Triad: Ophthalmoplegia, Identical Either demyelination or"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Areflexia, Ataxia to AIDP axonal degeneration Seen in 5% of cases Anti-GQ1b (Option D)"}]},{"n":4,"q":"A 35-year-old man presents with a 2-day history of progressive weakness that started in his legs after returning from his international trip. He has difficulty climbing stairs, increasing fatigue with normal activities, and mild tingling in his feet. He has a recent history of mild flu-like symptoms, including fever and malaise. On examination, he shows weakness in both lower extremities with reduced muscle tone and absent deep tendon reflexes. Laboratory findings reveal that CSF analysis showed elevated protein with a normal cell count. What is the most likely diagnosis?","o":[{"t":"Guillain-Barré syndrome"},{"t":"Tick paralysis"},{"t":"Myasthenia gravis"},{"t":"Poliomyelitis"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Answer: A) Guillain-Barré syndrome"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Explanation:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"The combination of progressive ascending weakness, absent deep tendon reflexes, recent flu-like symptoms, and CSF showing elevated protein with a normal cell count (albuminocytologic dissociation) is characteristic of Guillain-Barré syndrome."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Category Symptoms"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Motor Symptoms Progressive ascending paralysis (distal to proximal)"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Areflexia Weakness in legs causing rubbery legs and instability to stand Truncal paralysis, quadriplegia, neck floppiness Bilateral 7th nerve palsy Bulbar palsy Difficulty in handling secretions leading to aspiration pneumonia A leading cause of death due to pulmonary involvement. Diaphragmatic paralysis Ventilatory support is required in 30% of cases. Increased carbon dioxide levels."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Sensory Symptoms Glove-and-stocking distribution paresthesias"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Proprioception loss Difficulty in climbing stairs Pain in the neck/arm/shoulder is similar to over-exercised muscle Sensory manifestations can occur before or along with motor manifestations."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Autonomic Autonomic dysregulation (e.g., orthostatic hypotension, Symptoms bladder/bowel sparing/transient involvement)"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Wide fluctuations of blood pressure, arrhythmias, postural hypotension Reduced heart rate and blood pressure, neurogenic shock."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Tick paralysis (Option B) can cause ascending flaccid paralysis and is associated with recent tick exposure . The neurotoxins present in the tick's saliva act on the presynaptic nerve terminals, leading to a gradual onset of ascending flaccid paralysis . It also improves quickly with tick removal & presents with normal CSF findings unlike elevated protein in this case"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Myasthenia gravis (Option C): Causes fluctuating weakness that worsens with activity and is often accompanied by ocular symptoms (e.g., ptosis, diplopia). The acute onset and the pattern of weakness described do not match."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Poliomyelitis (Option D): Results in asymmetric weakness and has characteristic CSF findings of lymphocytic pleocytosis . The normal cell count and the symmetric weakness in this case are inconsistent with poliomyelitis."}]},{"n":5,"q":"A 40-year-old man presents with a 3-day history of progressive weakness, initially localized to his legs but now involving the arms. He reports increased difficulty with tasks such as buttoning his shirt and climbing stairs, along with episodes of fatigue during minimal exertion. He also describes a tingling sensation in his fingers and has experienced mild fever and generalized malaise over the past week. On examination, he demonstrates significant weakness in all four extremities, reduced muscle tone, and absent deep tendon reflexes. What is the next best step in the management of this patient?","o":[{"t":"Lumbar puncture for cerebrospinal fluid analysis"},{"t":"Nerve conduction studies"},{"t":"Serum acetylcholine receptor antibodies test"},{"t":"Polymerase chain reaction for poliovirus"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Answer: A) Lumbar puncture for cerebrospinal fluid analysis"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Explanation:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The suspected diagnosis is Guillain-Barré Syndrome."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"A lumbar puncture is essential for evaluating cerebrospinal fluid to identify elevated protein levels with a normal white blood cell count (albuminocytologic dissociation), which is characteristic of GBS."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Category Findings"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Lumbar Puncture CSF protein: 100-1000 mg/dL"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Elevated CSF protein but normal WBC count (albuminocytologic dissociation)"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"CSF Study Timing Normal CSF findings (<48 hours)"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Maximum rise of protein seen by 7 days"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Electrodiagnostic Testing Prolonged F-wave latencies"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Earliest finding in AIDP (Acute Inflammatory Demyelinating Polyneuropathy)"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Brighton Criteria for Guillain-Barré Syndrome (GBS)"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Mnemonic: FAME"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Criteria Description"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Flaccid Bilateral Weakness Weakness of limbs (flaccid)."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Areflexia Areflexia is present in areas of weakness."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"CytoAlbuminologic dissociation Elevated CSF protein with normal WBC count."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Monophasic Illness Symptoms develop over 12 hours to 28 days."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Electrodiagnostic Electrodiagnostic findings are consistent with"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"demyelination."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Miller Fisher Syndrome (MFS)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Areflexia without weakness"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Nerve conduction studies (Option B): are valuable for assessing nerve function but are typically not the first-line investigation."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Serum acetylcholine receptor antibodies test (Option C): is specific for diagnosing myasthenia gravis, which does not fit the clinical picture as DTR’s are spared."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Polymerase chain reaction for poliovirus (Option D): is relevant for diagnosing poliomyelitis and not applicable in this context."}]},{"n":6,"q":"A 50-year-old man presents with generalized weakness and inability to walk due to loss of balance after a recent viral illness. He reports double vision, and examination reveals bilateral ptosis and global areflexia. He has a history of alcohol use but is non-diabetic and non-hypertensive. What is the most appropriate treatment?","o":[{"t":"Intravenous immunoglobulin"},{"t":"Corticosteroids"},{"t":"Thiamine"},{"t":"Pyridostigmine"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Answer: A) Intravenous immunoglobulin"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Explanation:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The diagnosis is Miller-Fisher Syndrome (a variant of GBS), characterized by the classic triad of ophthalmoplegia (ptosis, double vision), ataxia (lack of coordination), and areflexia (absence of tendon reflexes)."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"IVIG is the first-line treatment, targeting the underlying autoimmune process."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Treatment Details"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"IVIG Treatment of choice (TOC)"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Plasmapheresis (Plex) Equally effective as IVIG; use either"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Functional improvement may occur >1 week after treatment or show delayed response"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Glucocorticoids Not useful in this condition (Option B)"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Monitoring Frequent assessment of maximal negative inspiratory"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"force (NIF) and vital capacity Determines need for ICU admission due to impending respiratory failure"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Rehabilitation Aggressive physical rehabilitation is imperative"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Thiamine supplementation (Option C): is for Wernicke's encephalopathy, not the autoimmune condition."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Pyridostigmine (Option D): is used for myasthenia gravis, not for this condition."}]},{"n":7,"q":"A 20-year-old female presents with acute dysphagia, particularly for liquids, and nasal regurgitation, following a recent dengue fever. Examination reveals bilateral facial weakness and areflexia in the upper limbs. Brain and cervical spine MRI is normal, while cerebrospinal fluid analysis shows elevated protein with normal glucose. What is the most likely diagnosis?","o":[{"t":"Acute pharyngeal cervicobrachial neuropathy"},{"t":"Miller-Fisher syndrome"},{"t":"Multiple sclerosis"},{"t":"Botulism"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Answer: A) Acute pharyngeal cervicobrachial neuropathy"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Explanation:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The diagnosis is Acute pharyngeal cervicobrachial Neuropathy ."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"This condition is characterized by oropharyngeal and cervicobrachial weakness, often with areflexia in the upper limbs. It is triggered by viral infections like dengue. The normal MRI findings and elevated protein in cerebrospinal fluid further support this diagnosis."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Pharyngeal-Cervical-Brachial (PCB) Variant of Guillain–Barré Syndrome"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Description: Rare localized variant of Guillain–Barré syndrome."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Aetiology: Associated with anti-GT1a antibodies."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Onset: Paralysis typically begins in the arms."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Clinical Features: Rapidly progressive oropharyngeal and cervicopharyngeal weakness. Areflexia in the upper limbs. Affects neck muscles and throat."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Rapidly progressive oropharyngeal and cervicopharyngeal weakness."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Areflexia in the upper limbs."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Affects neck muscles and throat."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Diagnosis: Features Required for Diagnosis: Symmetrical weakness in the oropharyngeal, neck, and arms, with areflexia. Absence of ataxia, altered consciousness, and significant leg weakness. A monophasic illness with a clinical plateau occurring within 12 hours to 28 days after onset. Exclusion of alternative diagnoses like brainstem stroke, botulism, and myasthenia gravis. Features Strongly Supportive of the Diagnosis: Antecedent infection (upper respiratory or gastrointestinal symptoms). CSF albuminocytological dissociation (high protein with normal cell count). Neurophysiological evidence of neuropathy Presence of IgG anti-GT1a or anti-GQ1b antibodies"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Features Required for Diagnosis: Symmetrical weakness in the oropharyngeal, neck, and arms, with areflexia. Absence of ataxia, altered consciousness, and significant leg weakness. A monophasic illness with a clinical plateau occurring within 12 hours to 28 days after onset. Exclusion of alternative diagnoses like brainstem stroke, botulism, and myasthenia gravis."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Symmetrical weakness in the oropharyngeal, neck, and arms, with areflexia."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Absence of ataxia, altered consciousness, and significant leg weakness."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"A monophasic illness with a clinical plateau occurring within 12 hours to 28 days after onset."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Exclusion of alternative diagnoses like brainstem stroke, botulism, and myasthenia gravis."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Features Strongly Supportive of the Diagnosis: Antecedent infection (upper respiratory or gastrointestinal symptoms). CSF albuminocytological dissociation (high protein with normal cell count). Neurophysiological evidence of neuropathy Presence of IgG anti-GT1a or anti-GQ1b antibodies"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Antecedent infection (upper respiratory or gastrointestinal symptoms)."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"CSF albuminocytological dissociation (high protein with normal cell count)."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Neurophysiological evidence of neuropathy"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Presence of IgG anti-GT1a or anti-GQ1b antibodies"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Treatment: Conservative Management: Supportive care and close monitoring, without the need for IVIg or plasmapheresis, in patients with no respiratory compromise or rapid progression IVIg or Plasmapheresis: These treatments are typically reserved for severe or rapidly progressing cases of GBS."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Conservative Management: Supportive care and close monitoring, without the need for IVIg or plasmapheresis, in patients with no respiratory compromise or rapid progression"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"IVIg or Plasmapheresis: These treatments are typically reserved for severe or rapidly progressing cases of GBS."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"These treatments are typically reserved for severe or rapidly progressing cases of GBS."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Monitoring and Follow-Up: Close monitoring of respiratory function is essential in GBS, especially with bulbar involvement. Follow-up at 4 weeks showed recovery of deep tendon reflexes and normalization of motor function ."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Close monitoring of respiratory function is essential in GBS, especially with bulbar involvement."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Follow-up at 4 weeks showed recovery of deep tendon reflexes and normalization of motor function ."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Rapidly progressive oropharyngeal and cervicopharyngeal weakness."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Areflexia in the upper limbs."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Affects neck muscles and throat."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Features Required for Diagnosis: Symmetrical weakness in the oropharyngeal, neck, and arms, with areflexia. Absence of ataxia, altered consciousness, and significant leg weakness. A monophasic illness with a clinical plateau occurring within 12 hours to 28 days after onset. Exclusion of alternative diagnoses like brainstem stroke, botulism, and myasthenia gravis."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Symmetrical weakness in the oropharyngeal, neck, and arms, with areflexia."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Absence of ataxia, altered consciousness, and significant leg weakness."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"A monophasic illness with a clinical plateau occurring within 12 hours to 28 days after onset."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Exclusion of alternative diagnoses like brainstem stroke, botulism, and myasthenia gravis."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Features Strongly Supportive of the Diagnosis: Antecedent infection (upper respiratory or gastrointestinal symptoms). CSF albuminocytological dissociation (high protein with normal cell count). Neurophysiological evidence of neuropathy Presence of IgG anti-GT1a or anti-GQ1b antibodies"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Antecedent infection (upper respiratory or gastrointestinal symptoms)."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"CSF albuminocytological dissociation (high protein with normal cell count)."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Neurophysiological evidence of neuropathy"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Presence of IgG anti-GT1a or anti-GQ1b antibodies"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Symmetrical weakness in the oropharyngeal, neck, and arms, with areflexia."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Absence of ataxia, altered consciousness, and significant leg weakness."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"A monophasic illness with a clinical plateau occurring within 12 hours to 28 days after onset."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Exclusion of alternative diagnoses like brainstem stroke, botulism, and myasthenia gravis."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Antecedent infection (upper respiratory or gastrointestinal symptoms)."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"CSF albuminocytological dissociation (high protein with normal cell count)."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Neurophysiological evidence of neuropathy"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Presence of IgG anti-GT1a or anti-GQ1b antibodies"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Conservative Management: Supportive care and close monitoring, without the need for IVIg or plasmapheresis, in patients with no respiratory compromise or rapid progression"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"IVIg or Plasmapheresis: These treatments are typically reserved for severe or rapidly progressing cases of GBS."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"These treatments are typically reserved for severe or rapidly progressing cases of GBS."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"These treatments are typically reserved for severe or rapidly progressing cases of GBS."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Close monitoring of respiratory function is essential in GBS, especially with bulbar involvement."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Follow-up at 4 weeks showed recovery of deep tendon reflexes and normalization of motor function ."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Miller Fisher syndrome (Option B): typically presents with a triad of ophthalmoplegia, ataxia, and areflexia."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Multiple sclerosis (Option C) usually shows distinct clinical features like visual disturbances and sensory deficits, and MRI would typically reveal lesions ."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Botulism (Option D): the specific findings of elevated protein with normal glucose in the CSF suggest PCB over botulism."}]},{"n":8,"q":"A 75-year-old man presents with progressive memory loss, difficulty in performing daily activities, and mood changes. His family notes that he often repeats the same questions and becomes easily confused. Neurological examination reveals no focal deficits, but cognitive testing indicates significant impairment in memory and language skills. What is the possible neurotransmitter status for this condition?","o":[{"t":"Increased Acetylcholine, decreased Norepinephrine, decreased Serotonin"},{"t":"Decreased Acetylcholine, decreased Norepinephrine, decreased Serotonin"},{"t":"Normal Acetylcholine, increased Norepinephrine, normal Serotonin"},{"t":"Increased Acetylcholine, increased Norepinephrine, decreased Serotonin"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Answer: B) Decreased Acetylcholine, decreased Norepinephrine, decreased Serotonin."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Explanation:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The diagnosis is Alzheimer's disease, characterized by progressive memory loss and cognitive decline."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"There is a significant decrease in acetylcholine levels (hence, AchE inhibitor drugs are preferred for the treatment to increase Ach levels), which is critical for memory and learning. Additionally, norepinephrine and serotonin levels are often decreased."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Neurotransmitters involved in Alzheimer’s"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Neurotransmitter Location of Synthesis"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Decreased ACh Nucleus basalis of Meynert (forebrain)"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Decreased Norepinephrine Locus coeruleus (pons)"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Decreased Serotonin Dorsal raphe (brainstem)"}]},{"n":9,"q":"A 30-year-old man with Down syndrome is being monitored for cognitive decline due to his increased risk for Alzheimer's disease. Why is this patient at a higher risk for developing Alzheimer's disease?","o":[{"t":"Presenilin 1 gene mutation"},{"t":"Overexpression of the APP gene"},{"t":"Mutation in the ApoE4 gene"},{"t":"Release of pro-inflammatory cytokines and promoting insulin resistance"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Answer: B) Overexpression of the APP gene"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Explanation:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Individuals with Down syndrome have trisomy 21, meaning they have an extra copy of chromosome 21."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"With the extra copy of chromosome 21, individuals with Down syndrome have overexpression of the (Amyloid Precursor Protein) APP gene, leading to increased production of amyloid-beta (A-beta)."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"This excess amyloid-beta tends to form amyloid plaques in the brain, a hallmark of Alzheimer's disease."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Causes of Alzheimer's Disease (AD)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"It is a multifactorial condition associated with many risk factors"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The most significant factor is age (>65 years of age)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Cardiovascular diseases: strokes or vascular dementia"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Obesity: Release of pro-inflammatory cytokines and promoting insulin resistance (Option D ruled out)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Diabetes: Neuroinflammation and accumulation of beta-amyloid (A-beta)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Other: Traumatic head injury, depression, higher parental age at birth, smoking, family history of dementia, and increased homocysteine levels"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Traumatic head injury, depression, higher parental age at birth, smoking, family history of dementia, and increased homocysteine levels"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Genetic causes:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Traumatic head injury, depression, higher parental age at birth, smoking, family history of dementia, and increased homocysteine levels"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Chromosome Presenilin 2 gene defective 1"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Chromosome Presenilin 1 gene defective (Option A ruled out) 14"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Chromosome ApoE gene 19 ApoE4 allele: Higher risk of developing sporadic"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Alzheimer's disease (Option C ruled out) ApoE2 allele: Low risk"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Chromosome The amyloid precursor protein (APP) gene 21 Alzheimer's Disease in Down syndrome (extra copy of"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"chromosome 21 leads to overexpression of the APP gene) (Option B) Early-age-of-onset familial AD"}]},{"n":10,"q":"Which of the following neurodegenerative disorders is not classified as a tauopathy?","o":[{"t":"Alzheimer’s disease"},{"t":"Punch-drunk syndrome"},{"t":"Parkinson’s disease"},{"t":"Pantothenate kinase-associated neurodegeneration (PKAN)"}],"a":[2],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Answer: C) Parkinson’s disease"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Explanation:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Parkinson’s disease is not classified as a tauopathy. It is primarily associated with Lewy bodies, composed mainly of misfolded alpha-synuclein, not tau."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Tauopathies"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Tauopathies are neurodegenerative disorders characterized by the deposition of abnormal tau protein (abnormal hyperphosphorylation of tau protein) in the brain."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Causes:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Alzheimer's disease (Option A ruled out)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Parkinson plus syndromes: Progressive supranuclear palsy Impaired downward gaze – recurrent falls Corticobasal syndrome Asymmetric motor abnormalities and profound apraxia Alien limb phenomenon Frontotemporal dementia (Pick’s disease): Mutations in genes like MAPT (tau) , GRN , and C9orf72 , linked to abnormal protein accumulation (tau, TDP-43) Associated with early personality changes Symptoms: Frontal lobe : Personality changes, disinhibition. Temporal lobe : Language disturbances. Memory : Relatively preserved in early stages (differentiates from Alzheimer's)."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Progressive supranuclear palsy Impaired downward gaze – recurrent falls"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Impaired downward gaze – recurrent falls"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Corticobasal syndrome Asymmetric motor abnormalities and profound apraxia Alien limb phenomenon"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Asymmetric motor abnormalities and profound apraxia"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Alien limb phenomenon"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Frontotemporal dementia (Pick’s disease): Mutations in genes like MAPT (tau) , GRN , and C9orf72 , linked to abnormal protein accumulation (tau, TDP-43) Associated with early personality changes Symptoms: Frontal lobe : Personality changes, disinhibition. Temporal lobe : Language disturbances. Memory : Relatively preserved in early stages (differentiates from Alzheimer's)."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Mutations in genes like MAPT (tau) , GRN , and C9orf72 , linked to abnormal protein accumulation (tau, TDP-43)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Associated with early personality changes"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Symptoms: Frontal lobe : Personality changes, disinhibition. Temporal lobe : Language disturbances. Memory : Relatively preserved in early stages (differentiates from Alzheimer's)."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Frontal lobe : Personality changes, disinhibition."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Temporal lobe : Language disturbances."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Memory : Relatively preserved in early stages (differentiates from Alzheimer's)."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Chronic traumatic encephalopathy"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Pantothenate kinase-associated neurodegeneration (PKAN) (Option D ruled out) Neurodegeneration with brain iron accumulation MRI Brain - \"eye of the tiger\" sign (On T2W MR scanning, the anteromedial globus pallidus shows bilateral symmetrical, central foci of hyperintense signals surrounded by a zone of hypointensity) Motor disorder of extrapyramidal type with gait difficulty Parkinson-like symptoms (basal ganglia involvement) Neuropsychiatric dysfunction"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Neurodegeneration with brain iron accumulation"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"MRI Brain - \"eye of the tiger\" sign (On T2W MR scanning, the anteromedial globus pallidus shows bilateral symmetrical, central foci of hyperintense signals surrounded by a zone of hypointensity)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Motor disorder of extrapyramidal type with gait difficulty"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Parkinson-like symptoms (basal ganglia involvement)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Neuropsychiatric dysfunction"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Punch-drunk syndrome (Option B ruled out) Extensive exposure of boxers to neurotrauma Currently, it is called dementia pugilistica"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Extensive exposure of boxers to neurotrauma"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Currently, it is called dementia pugilistica"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Progressive supranuclear palsy Impaired downward gaze – recurrent falls"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Impaired downward gaze – recurrent falls"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Corticobasal syndrome Asymmetric motor abnormalities and profound apraxia Alien limb phenomenon"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Asymmetric motor abnormalities and profound apraxia"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Alien limb phenomenon"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Frontotemporal dementia (Pick’s disease): Mutations in genes like MAPT (tau) , GRN , and C9orf72 , linked to abnormal protein accumulation (tau, TDP-43) Associated with early personality changes Symptoms: Frontal lobe : Personality changes, disinhibition. Temporal lobe : Language disturbances. Memory : Relatively preserved in early stages (differentiates from Alzheimer's)."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Mutations in genes like MAPT (tau) , GRN , and C9orf72 , linked to abnormal protein accumulation (tau, TDP-43)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Associated with early personality changes"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Symptoms: Frontal lobe : Personality changes, disinhibition. Temporal lobe : Language disturbances. Memory : Relatively preserved in early stages (differentiates from Alzheimer's)."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Frontal lobe : Personality changes, disinhibition."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Temporal lobe : Language disturbances."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Memory : Relatively preserved in early stages (differentiates from Alzheimer's)."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Impaired downward gaze – recurrent falls"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Asymmetric motor abnormalities and profound apraxia"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Alien limb phenomenon"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Mutations in genes like MAPT (tau) , GRN , and C9orf72 , linked to abnormal protein accumulation (tau, TDP-43)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Associated with early personality changes"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Symptoms: Frontal lobe : Personality changes, disinhibition. Temporal lobe : Language disturbances. Memory : Relatively preserved in early stages (differentiates from Alzheimer's)."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Frontal lobe : Personality changes, disinhibition."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Temporal lobe : Language disturbances."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Memory : Relatively preserved in early stages (differentiates from Alzheimer's)."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Frontal lobe : Personality changes, disinhibition."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Temporal lobe : Language disturbances."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Memory : Relatively preserved in early stages (differentiates from Alzheimer's)."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Neurodegeneration with brain iron accumulation"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"MRI Brain - \"eye of the tiger\" sign (On T2W MR scanning, the anteromedial globus pallidus shows bilateral symmetrical, central foci of hyperintense signals surrounded by a zone of hypointensity)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Motor disorder of extrapyramidal type with gait difficulty"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Parkinson-like symptoms (basal ganglia involvement)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Neuropsychiatric dysfunction"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Extensive exposure of boxers to neurotrauma"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Currently, it is called dementia pugilistica"}]},{"n":11,"q":"A 60-year-old woman has been diagnosed with early-stage Alzheimer's disease. Her husband reports that she frequently struggles to remember recent events and has begun to repeat herself in conversations. Additionally, she has difficulty managing her finances and has gotten lost while driving to familiar places. Despite these issues, she insists that she is “just fine” and is not concerned about her memory. Which term best describes the unawareness of her cognitive decline?","o":[{"t":"Subjective cognitive decline"},{"t":"Anosognosia"},{"t":"Apraxia"},{"t":"Mild cognitive impairment (MCI)"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Answer: B) Anosognosia"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Explanation: Anosognosia is a condition in which a person is unaware of their own illness or cognitive decline. This lack of awareness can lead to a denial of any problems and is commonly observed in various forms of dementia, including Alzheimer's disease."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Clinical Features of Alzheimer's"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Short term memory is lost first"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Memory impairment (affected initially d/t early involvement of temporal lobe), followed by deficits in executive function, language, and visuospatial abilities"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Word-finding, organizational, and navigational issues"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Visual processing dysfunction (posterior cortical atrophy) or difficulty naming and repeating words"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Motor Symptoms: Corticobasal syndrome Shuffling gait, muscle rigidity, and slowness of movement"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Corticobasal syndrome"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Shuffling gait, muscle rigidity, and slowness of movement"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Mood Changes: Depression, anxiety, and social withdrawal are common in the early stages"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Subjective Cognitive Decline: Patients perceive a decline in memory or cognition before it is clinically diagnosed (Option A ruled out)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Mild Cognitive Impairment (MCI): (Option D ruled out) Memory loss becomes noticeable to others and is confirmed with tests Activities of daily life may still be possible"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Memory loss becomes noticeable to others and is confirmed with tests"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Activities of daily life may still be possible"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Daily Activity Interference: Cognitive problems start affecting tasks like managing finances, driving, and housekeeping. Some patients are unaware of their deficits (anosognosia) (Option B)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Cognitive problems start affecting tasks like managing finances, driving, and housekeeping."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Some patients are unaware of their deficits (anosognosia) (Option B)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Patients are likely to get lost while walking or driving but may still maintain social behavior and superficial conversation"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Apraxia: (Option C ruled out) Difficulty performing learned motor tasks (e.g., using utensils) and trouble with dressing, eating, or walking"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Difficulty performing learned motor tasks (e.g., using utensils) and trouble with dressing, eating, or walking"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Simple puzzles or geometric figures become difficult to solve"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Judgment and reasoning decline, leading to delusions, often involving theft, infidelity, or misidentification"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Behavior can alternate between disinhibition, aggression, or withdrawal"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Sleep and Behavior Disruption: Nighttime wandering and disturbed sleep patterns"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"End Stages: Patients become mute, bedridden, incontinent, and need complete assistance with daily activities. They may also experience hyperactive reflexes and myoclonic jerks"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Patients become mute, bedridden, incontinent, and need complete assistance with daily activities."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"They may also experience hyperactive reflexes and myoclonic jerks"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Corticobasal syndrome"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Shuffling gait, muscle rigidity, and slowness of movement"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Memory loss becomes noticeable to others and is confirmed with tests"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Activities of daily life may still be possible"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Cognitive problems start affecting tasks like managing finances, driving, and housekeeping."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Some patients are unaware of their deficits (anosognosia) (Option B)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Difficulty performing learned motor tasks (e.g., using utensils) and trouble with dressing, eating, or walking"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Patients become mute, bedridden, incontinent, and need complete assistance with daily activities."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"They may also experience hyperactive reflexes and myoclonic jerks"}]},{"n":12,"q":"A 74-year-old woman is brought to a memory clinic by her daughter, who reports increasing forgetfulness over the past year. The daughter notes that her mother often misplaces items, struggles to find the right words during conversations, and repeats questions she has just asked. Recently, she became lost while driving to her regular grocery store, which she has frequented for years. On examination, she has difficulty following a conversation, and signs of apraxia are present. There are no significant findings on routine laboratory tests, and an FDG-PET is ordered. Which finding in FDG-PET is most consistent with the diagnosis of this patient’s condition?","o":[{"t":"Hypometabolism in the posterior temporal-parietal cortex"},{"t":"Cerebral atrophy"},{"t":"Widened third ventricle"},{"t":"Detection of amyloid depositions"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Answer: A) Hypometabolism in the posterior temporal-parietal cortex"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Explanation:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Clinical manifestations strongly correlate with a diagnosis of Alzheimer’s disease. FDG-PET scans commonly reveal hypometabolism in the posterior temporal-parietal cortex. This pattern correlates with the cognitive deficits associated with Alzheimer’s disease, including memory loss and difficulties with language and visuospatial skills."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Investigating Alzheimer’s Disease"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Cognitive Assessment:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Bedside Mini-Mental State Examination (MMSE) or Montreal Cognitive Assessment (MoCA)"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Laboratory Tests:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Routine tests (CBC, CMP, TSH, Vitamin B12) to rule out other causes of cognitive impairment. No specific abnormalities for AD."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Histopathology:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Neurofibrillary tangles:"},{"k":"img","v":8371},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Hirano bodies:"},{"k":"img","v":8377},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Imaging:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The main purpose of imaging is to exclude other disorders, such as primary and secondary neoplasms, vascular dementia, normal-pressure hydrocephalus, and other degenerative disorders, such as frontotemporal dementia (FTD or the prion disorder Creutzfeldt-Jakob disease (CJD)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"CT: Cerebral atrophy, widened third ventricle (Options B & C ruled out)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"MRI: Atrophy in entorhinal cortex, medial temporal cortex, and hippocampi"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Amyloid PET detects amyloid depositions (Option D ruled out)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Fluorodeoxyglucose-PET (FDG-PET) reveals hypometabolism in the posterior temporal-parietal cortex (Option A)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Functional brain imaging techniques such as Positron Emission Tomography (PET), functional MRI (fMRI), and Single-Photon Emission Computed Tomography (SPECT) help in mapping patterns of dysfunction in smaller brain areas of the medial temporal and parietal lobes"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"EEG:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Typically normal in AD patients"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"CSF Biomarkers:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Decreased CSF Ab42"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Increased p-tau and t-tau (phosphorylated and total tau) levels"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Genetics:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Not routinely recommended unless for rare early-onset AD cases"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Note: Slowly progressive decline in memory and orientation, normal results on laboratory tests, and an MRI or CT scan showing only distributed or posteriorly predominant cortical and hippocampal atrophy are highly suggestive of AD."}]},{"n":13,"q":"A 75-year-old woman with moderate Alzheimer's disease has been on Donepezil for the past year. On routine follow-up, her cognitive abilities are declining further, and the physician decides to add another medication. Which treatment would be most appropriate to consider at this stage?","o":[{"t":"Rivastigmine"},{"t":"Memantine"},{"t":"Aducanumab"},{"t":"Galantamine"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Answer: B) Memantine"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Explanation:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"A common approach to Alzheimer's disease drug therapy is to initiate a cholinesterase inhibitor for a patient diagnosed with mild AD dementia and to add memantine when patients enter the moderate stage of the disease."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Treatment of Alzheimer’s disease"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Patient and caregiver education."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Neurotransmitter-based therapies"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Acetylcholinesterase inhibitors Donepezil Rivastigmine (Option A ruled out) Galantamine (Option D ruled out)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Donepezil"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Rivastigmine (Option A ruled out)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Galantamine (Option D ruled out)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"N-methyl-d-aspartate (NMDA) glutamate receptor blocker Memantine"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Memantine"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"A common approach to AD drug therapy is to initiate a cholinesterase inhibitor for a patient diagnosed with mild AD dementia and to add memantine when patients enter the moderate stage of the disease. (Option B)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Donepezil"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Rivastigmine (Option A ruled out)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Galantamine (Option D ruled out)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Memantine"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Therapies targeting amyloid-n"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Aducanumab MOA: Monoclonal antibody targeting the N-terminus of the Ab peptide Dosage: Intravenous infusion every 4 weeks (7 infusions in total) Should only be considered for treating MCI (Mild Cognitive Impairment) or early dementia due to AD (Option C ruled out) S/E: Vasogenic cerebral oedema, cortical microhemorrhages, and superficial siderosis"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"MOA: Monoclonal antibody targeting the N-terminus of the Ab peptide"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Dosage: Intravenous infusion every 4 weeks (7 infusions in total)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Should only be considered for treating MCI (Mild Cognitive Impairment) or early dementia due to AD (Option C ruled out)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"MOA: Monoclonal antibody targeting the N-terminus of the Ab peptide"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Dosage: Intravenous infusion every 4 weeks (7 infusions in total)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Should only be considered for treating MCI (Mild Cognitive Impairment) or early dementia due to AD (Option C ruled out)"}]},{"n":14,"q":"Which of the following is NOT part of the clinical triad of Normal Pressure Hydrocephalus (NPH)?","o":[{"t":"Ataxia"},{"t":"Urinary incontinence"},{"t":"Dementia"},{"t":"Visual disturbances"}],"a":[3],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Answer: D) Visual disturbances"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Explanation:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Visual disturbance is not part of the clinical triad. They are not typically associated with NPH."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Normal Pressure Hydrocephalus"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"The clinical triad includes:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Abnormal gait (ataxic or apractic) (Option A ruled out)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Dementia (usually mild to moderate) (Option C ruled out)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Urinary urgency or incontinence (Option B ruled out)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Shuffling gait with preserved arm swing"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Neuroimaging :"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Enlarged lateral ventricles (hydrocephalus) with little or no cortical atrophy"},{"k":"img","v":8384},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Radionuclide cisternography:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Delay in CSF absorption over the convexity"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Lumbar puncture :"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The opening pressure is in the high-normal range"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"CSF protein, glucose, and cell counts are normal"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Removal of 30–50 mL of CSF causes transient improvement in gait or cognition"}]}]);
